---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: the same five obligations, asked of a US
// non-citizen and of a US citizen. Inline for the same reasons as
// ThreeQuestions.astro — see that file's header.
//
// This diagram now renders directly under the post's OPENING section, "Who it
// was built for", where the thesis is stated — it is the first diagram a reader
// meets, not the last. The Aug 2026 restructure moved it there so the claim and
// its evidence arrive together.
//
// The five rows used to be transcribed from a single enumerating sentence in
// the post. That sentence no longer exists: the trim in b65d4a3 cut it, and the
// restructure did not restore it. THE ENUMERATION NOW LIVES ONLY HERE, in the
// same sense WritePropagation.astro's specifics do — deleting a row deletes the
// claim, because there is no prose left to fall back on. The order is that
// original sentence's order and must not be resorted:
//   identifier, registration, address, status, exit.
//
// Every row is still separately supported by prose elsewhere in the post, and
// those sentences are what a row must continue to match:
//   A-number      "built to be an identifier and nothing else — it follows a
//                  person through every change of status"
//   registration  DHS rule finalized June 2026: "effectively every non-citizen
//                 aged fourteen or over who stays thirty days to register, be
//                 fingerprinted, and be issued a number"
//                 https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/06/29/2026-13057/alien-registration-form-and-evidence-of-registration
//   address       "Form AR-11 requires a change of address within 10 days of
//                  moving (8 U.S.C. § 1305), and failing to file is a criminal
//                  offense"
//   status        "for students and exchange visitors, the Student and Exchange
//                  Visitor Information System (SEVIS) does the same job, with
//                  the school reporting on their behalf"
//   exit          "a permanent resident who gives up the status submits Form
//                  I-407, and USCIS is required by law to report it to the IRS"
//   footnote      8 U.S.C. § 1304(e), which now CLOSES "What proves it": carry
//                 duty for a
//                 registered non-citizen 18 or older, "No US citizen has a
//                 comparable duty to carry one."
//   SSN cell      "From 1946 to 1972, the SSN card carried 'NOT FOR
//                  IDENTIFICATION.'"
//
// Two things a future editor must NOT "fix":
//   - The citizen column is dashed and empty in four of five rows. That is
//     .dg-node--out, the same "present on the page, absent from the result"
//     treatment RegionGraphNYC uses for New Jersey. It is the argument. Filling
//     the cells with plausible near-equivalents (a passport, a voter roll, a
//     state DMV record) would be exactly the inference-by-proxy the post is
//     about, asserted as though it were structure.
//   - The citizen column is NOT entirely empty: row one holds the SSN, solid,
//     because the number does exist — it was just never built for the job.
//     Dashing it too would overstate the case.
//
// The carry duty (§ 1304(e)) is a footnote rather than a sixth row because the
// post raises it two sections earlier and does not include it in the list this
// diagram tabulates. Matching the enumeration exactly is what lets a reader
// check the picture against the paragraph.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// aria IDs are prefixed dg-asym-* — four diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 700 462"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-asym-title dg-asym-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-asym-title">
      Five federal obligations, asked of a US non-citizen and of a US citizen
    </title>
    <desc id="dg-asym-desc">
      Five rows — stable identifier, registration on arrival, change of address,
      status reporting, exit record — across two columns, US citizen and US
      non-citizen. The non-citizen column is filled in at every row: the
      A-number, mandatory registration and fingerprinting for anyone fourteen or
      over staying thirty days, Form AR-11 within ten days of moving under
      8 U.S.C. § 1305, status reporting through SEVIS, and Form I-407 on
      departure, which USCIS must report to the IRS. The citizen column holds
      only the Social Security number, a card that read NOT FOR IDENTIFICATION
      from 1946 to 1972; the other four cells are drawn dashed and empty,
      because no such obligation exists. A footnote adds that a registered
      non-citizen eighteen or older must carry the document at all times, and
      that no citizen has a comparable duty.
    </desc>

    {/* ---- column headers ---- */}
    <text x="334" y="24" class="dg-call">US citizen</text>
    <text x="577" y="24" class="dg-call">US non-citizen</text>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 34 H 694" />

    {/* ---- row labels ---- */}
    <text x="6" y="82" class="dg-call dg-call--start">stable identifier</text>
    <text x="6" y="154" class="dg-call dg-call--start">registration on arrival</text>
    <text x="6" y="226" class="dg-call dg-call--start">change of address</text>
    <text x="6" y="298" class="dg-call dg-call--start">status reporting</text>
    <text x="6" y="370" class="dg-call dg-call--start">exit record</text>

    {/* ---- row 1: the one cell the citizen column actually has ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="220" y="46" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="334" y="70" class="dg-label">SSN</text>
      <text x="334" y="90" class="dg-sub">“NOT FOR IDENTIFICATION”,</text>
      <text x="334" y="104" class="dg-sub">printed on the card 1946–1972</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="46" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="70" class="dg-label">A-number</text>
      <text x="577" y="90" class="dg-sub">an identifier and nothing else,</text>
      <text x="577" y="104" class="dg-sub">carried through every status change</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 2: registration on arrival ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="118" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="156" class="dg-note">none</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="118" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="142" class="dg-label">mandatory</text>
      <text x="577" y="162" class="dg-sub">fourteen and over, thirty days,</text>
      <text x="577" y="176" class="dg-sub">fingerprinted — DHS rule, 2026</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 3: change of address ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="190" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="222" class="dg-note">none — and no register</text>
      <text x="334" y="238" class="dg-note">to write it to</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="190" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="214" class="dg-label">Form AR-11</text>
      <text x="577" y="234" class="dg-sub">within 10 days of moving —</text>
      <text x="577" y="248" class="dg-sub">8 U.S.C. § 1305, a criminal offense</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 4: status reporting ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="262" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="300" class="dg-note">none</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="262" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="286" class="dg-label">SEVIS</text>
      <text x="577" y="306" class="dg-sub">students and exchange visitors,</text>
      <text x="577" y="320" class="dg-sub">reported by the school</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 5: exit record ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="220" y="334" width="228" height="64" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="334" y="366" class="dg-note">none — the IRS finds out,</text>
      <text x="334" y="382" class="dg-note">and tells no one else</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="460" y="334" width="234" height="64" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="577" y="358" class="dg-label">Form I-407</text>
      <text x="577" y="378" class="dg-sub">USCIS is required by law</text>
      <text x="577" y="392" class="dg-sub">to report it to the IRS</text>
    </g>

    <text x="6" y="428" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      And 8 U.S.C. § 1304(e): a registered non-citizen 18 or older must carry the document at all times.
    </text>
    <text x="6" y="444" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      No US citizen has any comparable duty.
    </text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Five things the US requires of a non-citizen, and the same five asked of a
    citizen. The dashed cells are empty on purpose: there is no citizen
    equivalent to put in them. The one that is filled holds the Social Security
    number, which carried NOT FOR IDENTIFICATION on the card until 1972.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
